<GuideLayout guide={guides[4]} heading={guides[4].name}>
  <div class="reference-answer"><p>Your property-tax bill can include taxes based on your property’s assessed value plus separate charges. Measure PFD would add a charge based on building square footage. Use this guide to understand your bill, see where PFD would fit, and find the records you need.</p></div>
  <section id="bill"><h2>What am I paying for on my property-tax bill?</h2><p>Look for three categories: the general property tax of 1% of net taxable value, charges for voter-approved debt, and direct assessments. Direct assessments are separate charges that are not based on your property’s assessed value.</p><p>For the 1% calculation, use the net taxable value printed on your bill, not your home’s listing price.</p></section>
  <section id="pfd"><h2>How much would Measure PFD add?</h2><p>Measure PFD would add $0.19 per taxable square foot of building area each year, before exemptions, for a proposed 14-year term.</p><p>For example, 1,600 taxable square feet would produce a $304 annual PFD charge before exemptions. That would be added to your existing property taxes. <a href="/measure-pfd-calculator/">Calculate your PFD tax increase</a> or <a href="/measure-pfd-exemptions/">check your PFD exemptions</a>.</p></section>
  <section id="sales-tax"><h2>Is Measure I part of my property-tax bill?</h2><p>No. Measure I is a sales tax on purchases. The 2018 ballot question proposed a three-quarter-cent sales tax for general City services. It is not calculated from a home’s size or assessed value. Our <a href="/measure-pfd-vs-measure-i/">Measure PFD and Measure I comparison</a> shows what each tax applies to and how its revenue can be spent.</p></section>
  <section id="records"><h2>What should I check on my bill?</h2><ol><li>Find your Assessor’s Identification Number and tax year on the bill.</li><li>Check your net taxable value, 1% general tax, voter-approved debt charges, and direct assessments separately.</li><li>Use the agency contact listed beside a direct assessment for questions about that charge. Ask the Assessor about assessed value, and the Tax Collector about billing and payment.</li></ol><p>Each tax has its own exemption rules. Qualifying for a PFD exemption would not automatically remove other charges from your bill.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Need an exact amount for your property?</h2><p>Check your official tax bill for current amounts, including school bonds and district assessments. Contact the agency listed beside a charge if you have questions about how it applies to you.</p></section>
</GuideLayout>
